achable step-by-step layout with numbered sections. The title at the top reads \"How to Deal With a Large Centipede in Your Home or Workplace\" in the signature wikiHow green header font with the small wikiHow logo and a subtitle reading \"Co-authored by Dr. Elena Vasquez, Entomologist — Last Updated: March 2026 — Verified\"\n\nThe article flows vertically through numbered steps, each with a wikiHow-style pastel illustration.\n\nStep 1 illustration shows a simple pastel drawing of a woman in a polo shirt discovering a very large centipede on a counter — her hands are raised in surprise, the centipede is enormous and takes up most of the counter, drawn in the slightly goofy wikiHow art style. Caption reads \"1. Stay calm. The centipede is more scared of you than you are of it. This is not true but it is what people say in these situations and it may help you psychologically. Do not scream. Screaming startles them and a startled centipede can move up to 40mph.\"\n\nStep 2 illustration shows the same woman now calmly on her phone, the centipede still on the counter watching her. Caption reads \"2. Open a food delivery app or locate the nearest source of buffalo wings. Traditional or boneless both work. The centipede does not have a preference. If you only have access to hot wings, these are acceptable but expect the centipede to eat more slowly due to the heat level. Do not substitute chicken tenders. They will know.\"\n\nStep 3 illustration shows the woman placing an open styrofoam takeout container of orange buffalo wings on the counter near the centipede, which is already turning its head toward the food with its antennae extended. Caption reads \"3. Place the buffalo wings within reach of the centipede. Do not hand-feed on your first encounter — trust must be established gradually. Position the container approximately 6 inches from its head and step back. You will know it is working when the antennae begin to move toward the wings.\"\n\nStep 4 illustration shows the centipede actively eating a buffalo wing, its mandibles working on the chicken, the woman watching from a comfortable distance with a slight smile. Caption reads \"4. Allow the centipede to eat undisturbed. An average large centipede will consume between 4 and 7 wings per feeding session. Do not attempt to take the container away before it is finished. Do not comment on how much it is eating. They are sensitive about this.\"\n\nStep 5 illustration shows the centipede in a relaxed curled position on the counter, clearly in a food coma, the empty styrofoam container beside it with just bones and sauce residue, the woman gently placing a small folded paper towel near it. Caption reads \"5. After feeding, the centipede will enter a dormant state lasting 2-4 hours. This is normal. It is not dead. Do not poke it. Use this window to clean up any sauce that has gotten on the counter, the merchandise, or yourself. The centipede will wake up and leave on its own, or it will stay. If it stays, proceed to Step 6.\"\n\nStep 6 illustration shows the same woman on a subsequent night, now sitting comfortably on a stool behind the counter, the centipede draped across the counter in a relaxed position, another open container of buffalo wings between them, the woman offering a wing directly from her hand, the centipede taking it gently. Their body language suggests this has been happening for weeks. Caption reads \"6. If the centipede returns, repeat Steps 2-5. By the third or fourth session a bond will form. You may begin hand-feeding at this stage. Name it if you want. We recommend something dignified. It has earned your respect.\"\n\nAt the very bottom, a small wikiHow tips box reads \"TIPS: Ranch dressing is acceptable but blue cheese is preferred. If wings are unavailable, mozzarella sticks are an emergency substitute but this will damage the relationship long-term. Most centipedes prefer flats over drums.\" A small warnings box reads \"WARNINGS: Do not introduce the centipede to other employees during the bonding phase. Jealousy can occur — from the centipede or the employees. Do not Google how many legs it has. The answer will upset you.\" A small footer reads \"Views: 2.4M — 97% of readers found this article helpful.\" No border, no frame, edge to edge."], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "value"}, "name": "value", "label": "value", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "值"}], "flags": {}, "id": 2, "type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline", "title": "Prompt", "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.19.0", "ue_properties": {"widget_ue_connectable": {}, "input_ue_unconnectable": {}, "version": "7.8"}, "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"}, "order": 7}, {"mode": 0, "outputs": [{"name": "CLIP", "slot_index": 0, "links": [2], "label": "CLIP", "type": "CLIP", "localized_name": "CLIP"}], "size": [385.94073486328125, 127.52528381347656], "pos": [-442.9340515136719, -722.9832763671875], "widgets_values": ["ernie-image-prompt-enhancer.safetensors", "flux2", "default"], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "clip_name"}, "name": "clip_name", "type": "COMBO", "localized_name": "CLIP名称"}, {"widget": {"name": "type"}, "name": "type", "type": "COMBO", "localized_name": "类型"}, {"widget": {"name": "device"}, "shape": 7, "name": "device", "type": "COMBO", "localized_name": "设备"}], "flags": {}, "id": 5, "type": "CLIPLoader", "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.3.45", "ue_properties": {"widget_ue_connectable": {}, "input_ue_unconnectable": {}, "version": "7.8"}, "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "CLIPLoader"}, "order": 8}, {"mode": 0, "outputs": [{"name": "MODEL", "slot_index": 0, "links": [21], "label": "模型", "type": "MODEL", "localized_name": "模型"}], "size": [360, 82], "pos": [-891.8553466796875, 429.2247619628906], "widgets_values": ["ernie-image-turbo.safetensors", "default"], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "unet_name"}, "name": "unet_name", "type": "COMBO", "localized_name": "UNET名称"}, {"wid